    "evidence_text": "Blind individuals often demonstrate enhanced nonvisual perceptual abilities.\n\nHow many images are in an auditory scene?\n\nAuditory Cortex Tracks Both Auditory and Visual Stimulus Dynamics Using Low-Frequency Neuronal Phase Modulation\n\nLip movements entrain the observers\u2019 low-frequency brain oscillations to facilitate speech intelligibility\n\nDuring continuous speech, lip movements provide visual temporal signals that facilitate speech processing.\n\nOur results emphasize the importance of visually entrained and attention-modulated rhythmic brain activity for the enhancement of audiovisual speech processing.\n\nCongruent Visual Speech Enhances Cortical Entrainment to Continuous Auditory Speech in Noise-Free Conditions.\n\nThe Natural Statistics of Audiovisual Speech\n\nTests of this idea have largely been confined to unimodal signals and natural scenes.\n\nBy artificially reinstating these temporal fluctuations, envelope-tracking activity is regained.\n\nThese changes in tracking correlate with intelligibility of the stimulus.\n\nA 40-Hz auditory potential recorded from the human scalp.\n\nComputer techniques readily extract from the brainwaves an orderly sequence of brain potentials locked in time to sound stimuli.\n\nFrequency modulation entrains slow neural oscillations and optimizes human listening behavior.\n\nSecond, does neural entrainment optimize performance of the perceptual system: Does human auditory perception benefit from neural phase reorganization?",
